WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.640
It’s November 11th and in one week a copy of&nbsp;
the U.S. Constitution is going up for auction.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.640 --> 00:00:09.120
Everyone expects it to draw the usual crowd of&nbsp;
wealthy collectors, but the crypto community has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.120 --> 00:00:14.240
another plan. They want to crowdfund enough money&nbsp;
to place a winning bid, and they’ll do it using a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.240 --> 00:00:20.160
DAO. There’s only one week until the auction, so&nbsp;
they need to move fast. On Friday the 12th, they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.160 --> 00:00:24.960
put up a website. Things are messy, with competing&nbsp;
code repositories, but on Saturday, they find&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.960 --> 00:00:29.440
some partners to help. Juicebox will help with&nbsp;
crowdfunding, SyndicateDAO will help with legal,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.440 --> 00:00:33.840
and FTX will help with exchange. The auction&nbsp;
house agrees to work with the DAO on Sunday,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.840 --> 00:00:38.480
but there’s a problem… Sotheby’s has to follow&nbsp;
“know your customer” rules, so the DAO needs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.480 --> 00:00:43.920
to partner with an LLC. Two members of the DAO&nbsp;
step up to form the LLC and bridge the crypto and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.920 --> 00:00:48.960
traditional finance worlds. It’s now Monday, and&nbsp;
things are really picking up steam. Ten thousand&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.960 --> 00:00:53.360
people are chatting in the Discord. The media&nbsp;
starts catching on. Packy McCormick writes a blog,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.360 --> 00:01:01.680
then CNBC covers it. Donations start flowing.&nbsp;
Tuesday leads to more big names signing on. Chris&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.680 --> 00:01:06.800
Dixon and other A16Z folks are supporting the&nbsp;
project now. On Wednesday, they break $20 million&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.800 --> 00:01:11.520
raised. It starts looking like this might actually&nbsp;
happen. Thursday is auction day, and people are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.520 --> 00:01:16.960
absolutely stoked. The DAO has over $40 million&nbsp;
dollars now. It’s chaos in the Discord, no one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.960 --> 00:01:21.840
knows who’s representing the DAO. Clearly both&nbsp;
bidders really want this. Brooke Lampley places&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.840 --> 00:01:28.400
the winning bid at $41 million dollars, but no&nbsp;
one knows who she’s bidding for. DAO backers are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.400 --> 00:01:34.320
claiming victory, but slowly, the truth leaks out.&nbsp;
The DAO lost. And the winner is revealed. It’s Ken&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.320 --> 00:01:39.840
Griffin, the founder of Citadel and arch-nemesis&nbsp;
of the WallStreetBets reddit. A crushing defeat.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.000 --> 00:01:46.560
The story of the ConstitutionDAO is useful because&nbsp;
it touches so many different aspects of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.560 --> 00:01:51.040
current web3 landscape. So let’s go through the&nbsp;
good parts of this, then talk about some of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.040 --> 00:01:56.240
bad parts, and finally close on the ugly, because&nbsp;
it gets really ugly here. First up, the good.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.240 --> 00:02:00.800
Well, it’s the Constitution, what’s not to like?&nbsp;
It’s an extremely meaningful historical document&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.800 --> 00:02:04.720
and the DAO only had two rules for what they&nbsp;
were going to do with it. It had to be displayed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.720 --> 00:02:09.360
publicly, and it had to have a plaque next to&nbsp;
it that mentioned the ConstitutionDAO’s website.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.360 --> 00:02:14.080
That’s a noble goal, and it’s a big part of what&nbsp;
led to the massive surge in support. The team&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.080 --> 00:02:18.080
also did a great job of making it clear that they&nbsp;
weren’t in this for financial gain. Even though&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.080 --> 00:02:21.920
they could have easily given themselves some&nbsp;
tokens for free at the start of the project,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.920 --> 00:02:26.400
they didn’t, which helped build trust in the&nbsp;
community. Now, to be fair, there’s still a world&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.400 --> 00:02:30.160
where the core contributors wind up benefitting&nbsp;
from the project. The team was planning on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.160 --> 00:02:34.720
submitting a proposal for compensation after the&nbsp;
auction that would have been voted on by the DAO,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.720 --> 00:02:39.440
but that certainly seems reasonable to me. And a&nbsp;
lot of the team members had other web3 projects,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.440 --> 00:02:43.760
and a high profile story like this could draw&nbsp;
attention to what they're working on. But again,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.760 --> 00:02:47.920
that’s totally reasonable. This approach led to&nbsp;
a very positive community developing around the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.920 --> 00:02:52.080
project. Although I didn’t contribute to the&nbsp;
project, I was in the Discord and could see&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.080 --> 00:02:56.320
that everyone was just having fun and excited to&nbsp;
be a part of a cool story. No one was trying to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.320 --> 00:03:01.600
inflate expectations, and the core team was very&nbsp;
clear that the DAO was merely a governance token&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.600 --> 00:03:05.840
and did not represent real ownership in the&nbsp;
Constitution. Packy McCormick put it really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.840 --> 00:03:09.760
well in his blog post when he said “If&nbsp;
you want to contribute, you should think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.760 --> 00:03:14.960
about it as purchasing a keepsake as opposed to&nbsp;
investing in an asset.” Even with this caveat,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.960 --> 00:03:19.280
I think the fact that money donated to the&nbsp;
ConstitutionDAO was going to fund the purchase&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.280 --> 00:03:24.880
of a real physical item is important here. Unlike&nbsp;
previous crypto projects, the ConstitutionDAO is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.880 --> 00:03:29.440
very easy to understand, even for people&nbsp;
who are new to crypto. At this point,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.440 --> 00:03:34.720
most people understand what an NFT is, they’ve&nbsp;
seen the crazy jpegs selling for millions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.360 --> 00:03:40.640
and heard about massive Sothebys auctions, but not&nbsp;
everyone agrees that these NFTs have value. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.640 --> 00:03:46.000
Constitution on the other hand has centuries of&nbsp;
history behind it and could actually generate cash&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.000 --> 00:03:51.360
flow, at least in theory. Although this wasn’t&nbsp;
the plan, some museums do charge entrance fees,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.360 --> 00:03:56.560
so it’s not crazy to come up with some sort of&nbsp;
fundamental value based on traditional financial&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.560 --> 00:04:00.800
modeling. Even though the project didn’t&nbsp;
pan out as planned, it definitely served&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.800 --> 00:04:06.400
as a good educational tool for sparking interest&nbsp;
in DAOs. The idea of a Decentralized Autonomous&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.400 --> 00:04:12.160
Organization is still very new to most people,&nbsp;
but the ConstitutionDAO helped spread the idea&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.160 --> 00:04:17.840
that lots of people could come together quickly&nbsp;
online to crowdfund a specific goal. And unlike&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.840 --> 00:04:23.200
traditional crowdfunding, DAOs have a built-in&nbsp;
voting mechanism. It’s still unclear where these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.200 --> 00:04:28.080
DAOs will go, or how useful they will be in the&nbsp;
future, but more people are aware of how they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.080 --> 00:04:31.920
work, and I think that’s a win for the team&nbsp;
behind this project. Obviously the hope here&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.920 --> 00:04:36.000
was to win the auction and propel the DAO into&nbsp;
the mainstream zeitgeist, and it doesn’t look&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.000 --> 00:04:39.600
like that will happen. But it’s still remarkable&nbsp;
that this project was able to break into the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.600 --> 00:04:44.800
mainstream media in just a few days. Crypto&nbsp;
broadly has been a hot topic for years now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.800 --> 00:04:49.760
but most of the attention comes back to price&nbsp;
movements. Everytime bitcoin hits a new all-time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.760 --> 00:04:54.800
high there will undoubtedly be a press cycle&nbsp;
covering it, and that’s fine if you own Bitcoin,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.800 --> 00:04:58.800
but I don’t think it really moves the needle&nbsp;
where it’s needed most, the developer community.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.800 --> 00:05:02.880
The ConstitutionDAO represents a step forward&nbsp;
precisely because there wasn’t an expectation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.880 --> 00:05:08.000
of massive overnight gain, so one of the few ways&nbsp;
to get more involved was to actually contribute to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.000 --> 00:05:12.240
the project in some way. If you’re a proponent&nbsp;
of crypto, this seems like a good development,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.240 --> 00:05:17.760
the ConstitutionDAO was basically a massive stress&nbsp;
test of the current web3 infrastructure and it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.760 --> 00:05:22.400
clearly exposed a bunch of areas for development.&nbsp;
Most of the problems, which we will get to in a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.400 --> 00:05:28.240
minute, do have solutions, and they are now much&nbsp;
easier to see for interested developers. Last, but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.240 --> 00:05:34.000
certainly not least, it is extremely good that no&nbsp;
money was stolen here. Crypto is still very much&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.000 --> 00:05:40.000
a wild west, and fortunes are made and lost every&nbsp;
day. The very first DAO, which was appropriately&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.000 --> 00:05:46.320
named just “The DAO” raised $150 million dollars&nbsp;
before quickly getting hacked back in 2016. Even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.320 --> 00:05:52.240
though web3 projects can offer very high levels&nbsp;
of security, there are still a ton of bad actors&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.240 --> 00:05:56.800
out there who can scam people, but that didn’t&nbsp;
happen here. Even though they lost the auction,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.800 --> 00:06:01.920
supporters will get their money back, and that’s&nbsp;
a good result all things considered. The stakes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.920 --> 00:06:06.480
were clearly pretty high with tens of millions&nbsp;
of dollars flowing into the DAO, but the team&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.480 --> 00:06:11.920
handled things responsibly and kept communication&nbsp;
flowing. But now, we have to turn to the bad side&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:11.920 --> 00:06:16.320
of this project, and we should probably start&nbsp;
with the actual structure of the DAO, because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.320 --> 00:06:21.920
it’s extremely convoluted. Web3 projects tend to&nbsp;
work best when they are off in their own little&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.920 --> 00:06:27.440
pristine walled gardens. The world of traditional&nbsp;
finance is extremely messy and highly regulated,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:27.440 --> 00:06:32.320
but wading through all of that was unavoidable for&nbsp;
this project. Let me walk you through how money&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.320 --> 00:06:38.320
was actually going to flow from individuals to&nbsp;
the original owner of the constitution: Step one:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:38.320 --> 00:06:44.080
a contributor goes to a cryptocurrency exchange&nbsp;
like Coinbase and buys ether with U.S Dollars from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.080 --> 00:06:50.160
their bank account. Step two: they now send that&nbsp;
ether from Coinbase to a wallet like Metamask.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.160 --> 00:06:55.600
Step three: They use metamask to send ether to&nbsp;
the ConstitutionDAO project, which is hosted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:55.600 --> 00:07:00.960
by a crowdfunding platform called Juicebox. Step&nbsp;
four: all of the ether donations are collected in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.960 --> 00:07:05.840
a multi-sig wallet, which requires agreement&nbsp;
from 13 different team members in order to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:05.840 --> 00:07:10.880
move that money further. Step five: because&nbsp;
Sotheby’s can’t work directly with the DAO,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:10.880 --> 00:07:16.400
a Delaware LLC with just two signatories, has&nbsp;
to interface with the auction house. Step six:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:16.400 --> 00:07:20.560
The LLC would orchestrate the transfer to&nbsp;
Sotheby’s in the event that they won the auction.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.360 --> 00:07:25.360
That’s a remarkably complex process for&nbsp;
something pretty simple at its core.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:25.360 --> 00:07:29.200
We’re just talking about a bunch of people&nbsp;
pooling their resources to buy a single object,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:29.200 --> 00:07:34.960
this shouldn’t be so complicated, and yet it is,&nbsp;
because web3 is so new and best practices are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:34.960 --> 00:07:40.160
still being developed on the fly. One of the big&nbsp;
benefits of crypto projects is decentralization&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:40.160 --> 00:07:44.880
and the ConstitutionDAO was going to allow&nbsp;
everyone who donated to vote on where to display&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:44.880 --> 00:07:49.200
the constitution in proportion to the amount&nbsp;
they paid. The tokens they issued would allow&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:49.200 --> 00:07:53.920
these votes to take place without needing any&nbsp;
single person to be trusted to count the votes.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:53.920 --> 00:07:58.800
This is a great use for crypto and it wouldn’t&nbsp;
have any issues if there wasn’t a physical asset&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.800 --> 00:08:03.760
involved. The problem is that the further you get&nbsp;
from the blockchain, the more centralization is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:03.760 --> 00:08:09.120
required to move things forward quickly, and at a&nbsp;
certain point, the ConstitutionDAO wound up in a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:09.120 --> 00:08:14.400
situation where you basically had to trust that&nbsp;
the two people responsible for the Delaware LLC&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:14.400 --> 00:08:19.200
would do the right thing. I think these issues can&nbsp;
be solved though, especially with a long enough&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:19.200 --> 00:08:23.760
time horizon. It’s remarkable that the team was&nbsp;
able to do as much as they did in just seven days,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:23.760 --> 00:08:27.600
and I really have no doubt that we will see&nbsp;
much stronger patterns develop in the next&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:27.600 --> 00:08:33.200
few years here. But we still need to talk about&nbsp;
the most obvious bad outcome here: they lost. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:33.200 --> 00:08:38.000
DAO originally thought that raising $20 million&nbsp;
dollars would be enough, since that’s how much&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:38.000 --> 00:08:42.400
experts thought this copy of the constitution&nbsp;
was worth. The DAO wound up raising more than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:42.400 --> 00:08:48.400
twice that much, but they still couldn’t win, and&nbsp;
a big reason why is because the funding total was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:48.400 --> 00:08:54.000
public. Given that the maximum possible bid was&nbsp;
known to everyone, the seller of the constitution,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:54.000 --> 00:08:59.440
meaning the person who owned it and put it up for&nbsp;
auction, could bid against the DAO directly to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:59.440 --> 00:09:04.720
drive up the price. If the seller knows that the&nbsp;
DAO is willing to pay up to $40 million dollars,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:04.720 --> 00:09:10.400
they will bid up to $39 million just to get the&nbsp;
maximum payday from the DAO. Even if the DAO does&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:10.400 --> 00:09:16.400
win the auction, the more money they raise, the&nbsp;
more expensive the final price gets. Now, to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:16.400 --> 00:09:20.960
fair, it doesn’t look like the seller was bidding&nbsp;
on the Constitution at all. Ken Griffin is just a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:20.960 --> 00:09:25.440
multi-billionaire and was able to outbid the DAO&nbsp;
directly. But it’s always a bad idea to show your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:25.440 --> 00:09:30.000
cards before you have to, and that’s exactly&nbsp;
what the ConstitutionDAO did here. Even still,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:30.000 --> 00:09:34.560
in the long term, there could be a solution to&nbsp;
this problem. There is a branch of crypto called&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:34.560 --> 00:09:39.680
zero-knowledge proofs that could obfuscate&nbsp;
the exact amount of money raised by the DAO,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:39.680 --> 00:09:44.160
while still giving Sotheby's confirmation&nbsp;
that the DAO could fund their current bid.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:44.160 --> 00:09:48.560
It’s complex stuff, and it’s not going to be&nbsp;
implemented next week, but it’s theoretically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:48.560 --> 00:09:52.880
possible and would make a lot of sense for&nbsp;
the next project of this kind. The last bad&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:52.880 --> 00:09:57.120
outcome from this project has been a bit of a&nbsp;
punchline on Twitter: transaction fees. Because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:57.120 --> 00:10:02.960
of all the interest in web3 projects, the ethereum&nbsp;
blockchain has become very expensive to work with.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:02.960 --> 00:10:08.720
Everytime you go to execute a transaction, you’ll&nbsp;
usually get hit with a “gas fee” somewhere between&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:08.720 --> 00:10:14.080
$20 and $100 dollars. Even though the DAO lost the&nbsp;
auction, everyone that contributed still had to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:14.080 --> 00:10:19.120
pay gas fees to transfer their ether into the DAO.&nbsp;
Some people were estimating that the total cost&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:19.120 --> 00:10:25.440
of transaction fees could be as high as $5 million&nbsp;
dollars, but it looks like it was closer to $880k.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:25.440 --> 00:10:29.520
That’s still a ton of money though and it&nbsp;
doesn’t really fit with the long-term promise&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:29.520 --> 00:10:34.400
of cryptocurrency. Worse still, everyone who put&nbsp;
money in, would now be hit with more gas fees on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:34.400 --> 00:10:41.200
the way out as they requested refunds. The median&nbsp;
contribution size was only $200, so lots of people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:41.200 --> 00:10:46.480
wound up paying nearly 50% in transaction fees.&nbsp;
Everyone in the crypto community is aware of this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:46.480 --> 00:10:51.280
problem though and they know that this will hurt&nbsp;
adoption if it’s not fixed. The Ethereum team is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:51.280 --> 00:10:56.400
working on eth2.0 to lower these fees, and other&nbsp;
blockchains like Solana already offer low fees&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:56.400 --> 00:11:01.600
and similar functionality. So transaction fees are&nbsp;
unlikely to be a major problem in the long term,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:01.600 --> 00:11:05.920
even if they won’t be resolved overnight. But now&nbsp;
we have to discuss the really ugly truth about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:05.920 --> 00:11:11.120
this project. The core concept here is remarkably&nbsp;
simple. We’re just talking about a bunch of people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:11.120 --> 00:11:15.760
chipping in to buy something expensive, it’s not&nbsp;
complicated. And yet there still doesn’t seem to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:15.760 --> 00:11:20.880
be an easy way to get this done. Why is that? Part&nbsp;
of this has to do with trade-offs. There are good&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:20.880 --> 00:11:25.120
reasons for know your customer and anti-money&nbsp;
laundering laws, and it’s also important to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:25.120 --> 00:11:29.760
protect people from scams, but it seems ridiculous&nbsp;
to me that this is still so difficult in the year&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:29.760 --> 00:11:34.960
2021. We’re not talking about curing cancer here,&nbsp;
or building a mars colony, it’s really just moving&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:34.960 --> 00:11:39.840
some numbers around that represent value. When I&nbsp;
think about the future, I still envision all the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:39.840 --> 00:11:46.400
classic sci-fi elements: flying cars, interstellar&nbsp;
travel, immersive virtual environments. These&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:46.400 --> 00:11:52.080
crypto projects are a reminder that we still have&nbsp;
a remarkably messy financial system and dealing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:52.080 --> 00:11:57.120
with that takes time. I do think this is important&nbsp;
work, and I think that the ConstitutionDAO could&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:57.120 --> 00:12:01.680
lead to some meaningful innovation, but it’s&nbsp;
still a bit sad that we have to spend so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:01.680 --> 00:12:06.640
much time untangling messy systems, just to move&nbsp;
forward. I’m definitely going to do more research&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:06.640 --> 00:12:11.520
on crypto and Web3, so if you’d like to follow&nbsp;
along, just drop your email at johncoogan.com,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:11.520 --> 00:12:20.960
and please check out this recommended video, the&nbsp;
YouTube algorithm thinks you’ll really like it.

